# TwinTack WordPress Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a WordPress project for TwinTack, featuring:
- **Custom Theme**: `themes/twintack2025` - A dual-purpose sports equipment theme (baseball/fishing)
- **Custom Plugin**: `plugins/twintack-grip-manager` - Grip customization and order management system
- **E-commerce**: WooCommerce integration with custom features
- **Third-party integrations**: Monday.com, Make.com, Klaviyo, Gravity Forms

## WordPress Standards & Best Practices

### PHP Development
- Follow WordPress PHP Coding Standards
- Use WordPress hooks and filters (no direct core modifications)
- Prefix all functions with `twintack_` to avoid conflicts
- Use class-based architecture for major components (singleton pattern preferred)
- Always escape output using `esc_html()`, `esc_attr()`, `wp_kses()`, etc.
- Sanitize all user inputs using appropriate WordPress functions
- Use WordPress database functions (`get_post_meta()`, `update_post_meta()`, etc.)
- Document all functions and classes with PHPDoc comments

### File Structure Conventions
- **Theme PHP classes**: `/inc/` with naming `class-{name}.php`
- **Theme template parts**: `/template-parts/` organized by functionality
- **Theme templates**: `/templates/` with naming `template-{name}.php`
- **Plugin classes**: `/includes/` with naming `class-grip-{name}.php`
- **WooCommerce overrides**: `/woocommerce/` maintaining WooCommerce directory structure

### Database & Custom Post Types
- Use custom post types for specialized content (grip-design, etc.)
- Prefix all custom meta fields with `_grip_` or `_twintack_`
- Use WordPress meta functions for data storage/retrieval
- Implement proper data validation and sanitization
- Use transients for caching expensive operations

## Theme-Specific Guidelines

### TwinTack Theme (`themes/twintack2025`)
- **Main entry point**: `functions.php` - includes loader and core functionality
- **Class organization**: Use `/inc/` subdirectories by functionality (header/, team/, marquee/, etc.)
- **Template hierarchy**: Follow WordPress template hierarchy with custom templates in `/templates/`
- **Dual-category system**: Support both baseball and fishing product categories
- **Responsive design**: Mobile-first approach using Bootstrap 5.3.2
- **Font system**: Use Archivo Google Font as primary typeface

### Asset Management
- **CSS**: Use `/css/` directory, compile SASS if needed
- **JavaScript**: Use `/js/` directory, enqueue properly through WordPress
- **Images**: Optimize before adding, use SVG for icons when possible
- **Fonts**: Load Google Fonts through wp_enqueue_style()

### Component Architecture
- **Modular design**: Use template parts for reusable components
- **Class-based functionality**: Major features as PHP classes with singleton pattern
- **Hook-based integration**: Prefer hooks over direct function calls
- **Conditional loading**: Only load scripts/styles when needed

## Plugin-Specific Guidelines

### TwinTack Grip Manager (`plugins/twintack-grip-manager`)
- **Version**: Currently 1.6.01 - maintain semantic versioning
- **Main class**: `TwinTack_Grip_Manager` - singleton pattern
- **Dependencies**: Requires WooCommerce and Gravity Forms
- **File organization**: Use `/includes/` for classes, `/assets/` for CSS/JS, `/templates/` for template overrides

### Key Plugin Features
- **Custom post type**: `grip-design` for customer grip orders
- **Status system**: Separate WordPress post status from artwork status
- **Customer dashboard**: Integration with WooCommerce My Account
- **API endpoints**: REST API for Monday.com and Make.com integrations
- **Purchase-based creation**: Only create grip posts when orders are completed

### API Development
- **REST API**: Use WordPress REST API standards
- **Authentication**: API key authentication for external integrations
- **Error handling**: Comprehensive error responses with proper HTTP codes
- **Data validation**: Validate all API inputs thoroughly
- **Security**: Proper nonce verification for AJAX requests

## Integration Guidelines

### Third-Party Services
- **Monday.com**: Use asset IDs for mockup storage, support direct asset URLs
- **Make.com**: Webhook integration for status updates and notifications
- **Klaviyo**: Email marketing integration with proper API handling
- **Gravity Forms**: Form processing and data collection
- **WooCommerce**: Custom product features and checkout modifications

### WooCommerce Customization
- **Product variations**: Custom display logic for different product types
- **Cart modifications**: Custom metadata for grip designs
- **Checkout process**: Purchase-based grip creation workflow
- **Account pages**: Custom endpoints for grip design management
- **Order processing**: Hook into order completion for grip creation

## Development Workflow

### Before Starting Development
1. Check `claude notes/` directory for relevant reference materials
2. Review existing documentation in `themes/twintack2025/docs/`
3. Understand the feature in context of the overall system
4. Check for existing similar implementations in the codebase

### Code Standards
- **Debugging**: Use `WP_DEBUG` and WordPress debugging functions
- **Error logging**: Use `error_log()` with descriptive prefixes
- **Performance**: Minimize database queries, use caching where appropriate
- **Security**: Always validate, sanitize, and escape data
- **Compatibility**: Ensure WordPress 5.8+ and PHP 7.4+ compatibility

### Testing Requirements
- Test on multiple browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Test responsive design on mobile, tablet, desktop
- Verify WooCommerce integration works correctly
- Test user roles and permissions
- Validate form submissions and data processing

## Specific Implementation Patterns

### User Role Management
- Support multiple user types: customers, wholesale, affiliates
- Role-based pricing and product access
- Unified login system with appropriate redirects
- Custom user registration process

### Status Management
- **Grip designs**: Use artwork status separate from post status
- **Order workflow**: Track from submission to production to shipping
- **Customer communication**: Clear status labels and progress indicators
- **Admin management**: Comprehensive status controls

### Data Flow Patterns
1. **Form submission** → **Temporary storage** → **Payment** → **Post creation**
2. **Order completion** → **Webhook notification** → **Status update**
3. **Customer feedback** → **Make.com integration** → **Design revision**

## Security Considerations
- Always use WordPress security functions (nonce verification, capability checks)
- Sanitize all user inputs using WordPress functions
- Escape all output appropriately
- Use secure API key authentication for external services
- Implement proper permission checks for customer data access
- Follow WordPress security best practices for file uploads

## Performance Guidelines
- Use WordPress caching mechanisms (transients, object cache)
- Optimize database queries (avoid N+1 problems)
- Conditionally load assets (only when needed)
- Optimize images and use appropriate formats
- Minimize HTTP requests where possible

## Documentation Requirements
- Update relevant documentation when adding/modifying features
- Use PHPDoc comments for all functions and classes
- Document any non-obvious implementation decisions
- Update changelog files when releasing new versions
- Maintain API documentation for external integrations

## File Naming Conventions
- **PHP classes**: `class-{descriptive-name}.php`
- **Template files**: `template-{name}.php` or follow WordPress conventions
- **JavaScript files**: `{descriptive-name}.js`
- **CSS files**: `{descriptive-name}.css`
- **Asset files**: Use descriptive names, optimize for web

## Error Handling Standards
- Use WordPress error handling (`WP_Error` class)
- Provide meaningful error messages for debugging
- Log errors appropriately for different environments
- Handle API failures gracefully
- Provide user-friendly error messages in frontend

## When Working with Existing Code
- Review existing patterns before implementing similar features
- Maintain consistency with established coding styles
- Update related documentation when modifying features
- Consider backward compatibility when making changes
- Test thoroughly before deploying changes

Remember: This is a complex e-commerce system with multiple integrations. Always consider the impact of changes on the entire workflow from customer form submission to order fulfillment.

## Additional Specialized Rules

For comprehensive guidance, also reference these specialized rule files in `.cursor/rules/`:

- **api-integrations.cursor-rules** - Third-party API integration patterns (Monday.com, Make.com, Klaviyo, Gravity Forms)
- **data-workflow.cursor-rules** - Data structures, workflow patterns, and purchase-based grip creation
- **development-environment.cursor-rules** - FTP-only environment debugging and deployment strategies
- **README.md** - Complete documentation of all rule files and their usage

These specialized rules provide detailed guidance for specific aspects of the TwinTack build and should be consulted alongside these base rules.